SGHarlekin said:
You could just open your rpg_sprites.js in the js folder of your project, scroll to line 649 and change the motion directly. (Or for good practice, copy the function and put it in a new file, call it ActorMotions.js or whatever and use as a plugin. It's better to not edit core scripts directly.)
View attachment 351993
Not sure if it will still do a hiccup, but worth a shot.
as for using a skill, you could use yanfly's action sequence packs and just leave the <target action> / <whole action> empty. Then the sprite will just continues to idle.
Click to expand...
Thanks!! I tweaked the lines on a different .js file and also I saw down bellow  this other lines:
Sprite_Actor.prototype.refreshMotion = function() {
const actor = this._actor;
if (actor) {
const stateMotion = actor.stateMotionIndex();
if (actor.isInputting() || actor.isActing()) {
this.startMotion("walk");
} else if (stateMotion === 3) {
this.startMotion("dead");
} else if (stateMotion === 2) {
this.startMotion("sleep");
} else if (actor.isChanting()) {
this.startMotion("chant");
} else if (actor.isGuard() || actor.isGuardWaiting()) {
this.startMotion("guard");
} else if (stateMotion === 1) {
this.startMotion("abnormal");
} else if (actor.isDying()) {
this.startMotion("dying");
} else if (actor.isUndecided()) {
this.startMotion("walk");
} else {
this.startMotion("wait");
}
}
};
Which I´m guessing dictate when to refresh and start other motions, so I deleted  the chanting, guard, abnormal and dying and I think it helped quite a bit! thanks a mil! though it´d be great to know if these lines here were okay to tweak too, nothing seems to have been broken in my testing (?)
